The classification theorem matches the version where the obstruction is specifically the residue class \(2 \pmod k\), and the exceptional end-blocks depend on the parity of \(k\):

- if \(k\) is odd, every end-block must be isomorphic to a graph in \(\{K_{k+1},K_{k,k}\}\cup \mathcal H_k\);
- if \(k\) is even, every end-block must be isomorphic to \(K_{k+1}\);
- and every non-end-block contains no cycle of length \(2 \pmod k\).

This is exactly the statement in choice (D).

\boxed{D}
